{
  "_": [
    "The sanctioned material set. MATERIAL-SPEC §5.",
    "",
    "Every part in the game names a material from this file. It is required and there is",
    "no default, because a default would be a silent decision about how something breaks,",
    "and how things break is the game.",
    "",
    "Mass is derived — volume × density — so a stone block is heavy because stone is heavy,",
    "not because someone typed a number. FORMAT-SPEC §6's `mass` field is an override for",
    "the rare case where derived mass is wrong (a hollow crate, an empty fuel drum).",
    "",
    "Packs cannot add to this file. A pack defines a new material only by `extends` on a",
    "core material, clamped to ×0.5–×2.0 on any numeric property, with `class`, `failure`",
    "and `hardness` inherited and not overridable (MATERIAL-SPEC §8). Identity is a pack's",
    "to define; physics is not.",
    "",
    "Thirty-three entries. MATERIAL-SPEC §5 says thirty, and its five tables do total",
    "thirty — but it then names `ice`, `glass` and `water` under 'Other' and points at",
    "EARTH-SPEC §8, which covers water only and gives no numbers for any of them. Those",
    "three are authored here and flagged `authored_here` so the gap is visible rather than",
    "looking like settled spec. §5's count wants amending to thirty-three."
  ],

  "format": 1,
  "id": "core",

  "damage_types": {
    "_": [
      "Six, closed set. MATERIAL-SPEC §3. Numbers everywhere in this file are *damage",
      "taken*, so lower is tougher — 0.15 means the material absorbs 85% of it."
    ],
    "order": ["kinetic", "blast", "crushing", "cutting", "fire", "dig"],
    "kinetic":  "bullets, arrows, fragments",
    "blast":    "explosive overpressure",
    "crushing": "rams, falling debris, direct artillery",
    "cutting":  "blades, saws, cutters",
    "fire":     "burning",
    "dig":      "tools"
  },

  "failure_modes": {
    "_": ["Closed set. What breaking looks like, and what it leaves."],
    "order": ["shatter", "crumble", "splinter", "tear", "deform", "burn_through"]
  },

  "hardness_tiers": {
    "_": [
      "MATERIAL-SPEC §4. A tool declares `tool_power` and works material where",
      "`tool_power >= hardness`.",
      "",
      "A tool that cannot do the job says so — 'your shovel won't cut chalk, you need a",
      "pick.' Never a silent no-op, never an animation that plays and achieves nothing.",
      "That refusal message is the entire cost of tool gating and it is cheap to pay.",
      "",
      "This is also what gives each era its own texture in how it breaks the world, with",
      "no era-specific code anywhere: ancient does everything by hand and by fire,",
      "gunpowder brings the charge, modern brings the drill and the torch."
    ],
    "0": { "worked_by": ["hands"] },
    "1": { "worked_by": ["shovel"] },
    "2": { "worked_by": ["shovel_slow", "pick", "axe"] },
    "3": { "worked_by": ["pick", "sledge", "cutter"] },
    "4": { "worked_by": ["drill", "charge"] },
    "5": { "worked_by": ["charge", "torch"] }
  },

  "classes": {
    "_": [
      "Class defaults, overridable per material. MATERIAL-SPEC §3.",
      "",
      "Read a resist row across and the material's personality is right there. Stone",
      "shrugs off a blade and shatters under a hammer. Earth swallows bullets and is",
      "trivially dug. Fabric stops almost nothing but a knife goes through it like it",
      "isn't there.",
      "",
      "`other` deliberately has no resist row — §3's table lists five classes, not six.",
      "So an `other` material must state all six resistances itself, and the validator",
      "refuses one that doesn't. Better than inventing a sixth row nobody specified."
    ],

